# plpgsql-known-failures.allow — ratchet ledger for upstream's *supplementary*
# PL/pgSQL suites (vendor/postgresql/src/pl/plpgsql/src). Companion:
# regress/run-plpgsql-regress-docker.sh. Precedent: regress/known-failures.allow
# and regress/contrib-known-failures.allow.
#
# Semantics are the other two ledgers': a listed test may fail without failing
# CI; an unlisted test that fails breaks the build; a listed test that starts
# PASSING is stale and also breaks the build. The file can only shrink.
#
# SEED: 2026-09-05, first execution of these suites against pgrust
# (submodule REL_18_3 = 62d6c7d3df6287f1bd83199c1a746e50d31571a0, server built
# from main @ 96703051d5). Result: 5 passed, 8 failed, 0 harness errors.
# Every entry below was read before it was written; none is a raw count.
#
# Note what these suites add over the core suite's single 122 KB `plpgsql`
# test, which passes: procedure/transaction control, record and composite
# datums, domains over composites, and CONSTANT/NOT NULL variable properties.
# The failures cluster in exactly the area the core test does not reach.
#
# Clusters, for whoever picks these up:
#
#   composite / record datums (PLPGSQL_TTYPE_REC) — four of the eight, and
#     almost certainly one root cause:
#       plpgsql_trigger   "record type has not been registered" for row(old.*)
#       plpgsql_record    "cannot cast type record to two_int8s"
#       plpgsql_varprops  composite variables initialise to NULL, and CONSTANT
#                         is not enforced on a composite field assignment
#       plpgsql_domain    self-reported: "domain-over-composite variables
#                         (PLPGSQL_TTYPE_REC) unported — unit
#                         backend-pl-plpgsql-comp" (pl_comp.c:1998-2005)
#
#   error CONTEXT / message text — divergences in what is reported, not in
#     what happens:
#       plpgsql_cache     wrong statement kind in the CONTEXT frame ("at
#                         assignment" for "at RETURN"), and a missing
#                         "SQL statement" frame
#       plpgsql_misc      3-part %ROWTYPE name gives "improper qualified name"
#                         where C gives "cross-database references are not
#                         implemented"
#
#   CALL / procedures:
#       plpgsql_call        "cache lookup failed for function <oid>" when a
#                           procedure argument is itself a function call —
#                           reads as a genuine OID/cache defect, not unported
#       plpgsql_transaction self-reported: "stmt_call (pl_gram.y): DO unported
#                           — unit backend-pl-plpgsql-gram"

plpgsql_cache
plpgsql_call
plpgsql_domain
plpgsql_misc
plpgsql_record
plpgsql_transaction
plpgsql_trigger
plpgsql_varprops
